# Q: can a player choose where a structure is built, and is he told why not when he cannot?
#
# Run against the default skirmish, which is the map the feature is for:
#
#   MiVic.Game.exe --probe tools/probe/structure.probe --probe-out artifacts/probe/structure.txt
#
# A structure used to be produced by another structure and to appear at a fixed offset from
# whatever made it: a position the player did not choose, could not see before paying for it, and
# found out about by watching a building pop up somewhere they did not intend. It is now raised on
# a site the player picks, so the questions are the ones a click raises: would the game take this
# site, is the player told why not, and did the building come up where they aimed.
#
# The player's base is at (-248.4, -117.2) m — `base-ground.probe` is where those numbers come
# from. The refusals are the lake south-west of the base, the volcano in the north-east, and the
# two rules that are about the building rather than about the map: the ground it needs under its own
# footprint, and the ground another structure is already standing on.

# Frame the base, so the pixels below are on screen and the shots are of the ghost.
focus -240 -110
zoom 170
pitch -0.90

# The census first, because it is the question the whole rule exists to answer: how much of the
# ground around this base would take a factory at all. It used to be fifteen cells within a hundred
# metres, every one of them inside the base's own yard — a factory twenty metres west of the player's
# own headquarters was refused as ανώμαλο έδαφος for failing a test meant for a base — and it is now
# most of the ground that is ground. The line below breaks the refusals down by the sentence each one
# gets, so a change to the rule is measured rather than argued about: `footprint` is the ground rule,
# `standing` is what is already built there, and the rest is the map.
sites Factory -248.4 -117.2 100

# The simulation's own answer about a factory at five sites, before the interface is involved at
# all. Two accepted sites a player would choose, and three refusals that name their rule: water,
# lava, and something that is not a structure in the first place.
structure Factory -248.4 -89.1
structure Factory -254.7 -86.0
structure Factory -257.0 -210.0
structure Factory 136.0 -42.2
structure Tank -245.3 -95.3

# And the two refusals that are about buildings rather than about the ground. The first is the cell
# the headquarters itself stands on; the second is eighteen metres north of it, which is the site
# this script used to build on and which is now inside the headquarters' own footprint — a cell is
# 9.4 m, a building is 28 m of ground, and two of them eighteen metres apart are two buildings
# sharing a wall. The ground at both is fine, which is the point: these are not ανώμαλο έδαφος, and
# the player reads which rule refused them.
structure Factory -248.4 -117.2
structure Factory -245.3 -95.3

# Select the headquarters, which is the building whose panel carries the structure rows. The click
# is an ordinary selection: nothing is armed yet, so it picks what is under the cursor.
hover -248.4 -117.2
click
units soviet 1

# What pressing the Εργοστάσιο row does. This is the HUD's own command path, not a flag set by the
# script: a row that orders a building at an offset instead of asking for a site is the bug, and
# this line is where it would show.
arm Factory

# The cursor over ground the rule accepts, three cells north of the headquarters: the pixel a world
# point projects to, what the client resolves that pixel back to, and whether the ghost is green.
hover -248.4 -89.1

# The ghost is the building itself rather than a footprint rectangle, so a shot of it says what is
# being placed as well as where, and the panel carries the armed row.
hud on
shot artifacts/probe/structure-ghost-green.png

# The site inside the headquarters' footprint: the ghost goes red and the row in the panel says
# which rule refused it. The click that follows is refused in the same frame and leaves placement
# armed — the player is aiming, and a refusal that disarmed them would mean pressing the row again
# for every cell they try.
hover -245.3 -95.3
shot artifacts/probe/structure-ghost-occupied.png
click
structures 0

# Water, further out: the same red ghost, a different sentence.
hover -257.0 -210.0
hover -253 -199
shot artifacts/probe/structure-ghost-water.png
click
structures 0

# Lava, four hundred metres away: the same red ghost, a different sentence again. The camera moves,
# the cursor is put down again where the point now projects to, and the refusal is asked for at the
# new pixel.
focus 136.0 -42.2
zoom 120
hover 136.0 -42.2
hud off
shot artifacts/probe/structure-ghost-lava.png
click
structures 0

# And the site the player actually wants, three cells north of their own headquarters: accepted, an
# order issued, and a building site standing there on the next tick.
focus -240 -110
zoom 170
hover -248.4 -89.1
hud on
click

tick 1
structures 0

# Rising: a structure is a building site for the whole of its construction, which is what makes the
# site the player chose worth having chosen — they can see it going up where they put it.
tick 120
structures 0
hud off
shot artifacts/probe/structure-rising.png

tick 160
structures 0
shot artifacts/probe/structure-built.png

# The ledger: what the census above answered, and the site this seed gives for a factory north of
# the Soviet base. The number is the whole reason the census exists — the same box accepted fifteen
# cells before a building's footprint replaced a base's yard as the thing a structure was judged on
# — and cell 5,22 is where the building site was raised, rather than at an offset from anything.
expect "cells within 100 m of the Soviet base that take a factory" 81 81
expect "the factory's site stands at x -248.4 m" -248.4 -248.4 0.1
expect "the factory's site stands at z -89.1 m" -89.1 -89.1 0.1
expect "the factory takes 272 ticks to rise" 272 272
